WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.135
Speaker Kevin McCarthy speaking

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.703
just moments after the House

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:04.671
successfully passed

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:06.306
by a wide bipartisan margin.

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:07.540
A vote, a bill

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.909
to avert a debt crisis

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:10.944
here in the United States.

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:13.613
Gloria, you noted, as we were discussing

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:14.481
just a moment moments

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.682
ago, more Democrats

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:16.449
than Republicans

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:17.250
voting for this bill

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.885
in a Republican controlled House

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:19.919
of Representatives. Right.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.154
And I think that's

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:22.522
going to be a sore point

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.190
for a bunch of Democrats.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.359
The Progressive Caucus,

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.360
which didn't like this

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.561
bill, didn't ever take

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.630
an official position on it against it.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.732
But in the House, you had the

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.300
Republican Freedom Caucus

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.002
take a position against it.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.704
And so the Democrats held back a little

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.839
because they vote for debt ceilings.

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:42.042
They want to pay the bills.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.543
And there were Republicans

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.845
who voted for it. 149.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.982
And it sort of shows you how

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.484
the conservative Freedom Caucus

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.286
is an island

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:53.853
in the

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:54.988
in the Republican Party

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.523
in the House right now.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:59.059
And I think that most Republicans

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:00.360
said, you know, we have to do this

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.462
because we are now a party

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.196
that has to show

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.365
that it can govern in the House.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.100
And used to be the big concern

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.401
when it came to debt

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.536
ceiling fights of which

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.137
we've had almost every two to three years

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.205
for the last decade,

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:13.440
except to remind me

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.208
exactly when they voted for a two year

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:15.775
suspension

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.177
of the conversation altogether.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.178
I think what's interesting

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:20.447
is hearing McCarthy there say

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:22.215
he's talking about the negotiation

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.116
and who came to the table

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:24.617
and who didn't come to the table.

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.319
He's not saying we held strong

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.021
until we got this, this and that

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.956
because he's trying to show

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.624
that they are engaging,

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.692
they are governing.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:33.660
And I think the lesson of

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.395
the last couple of years has been

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:37.030
if you take it to the limit

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.598
on one of these fights,

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.233
you don't win with the public.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.634
They don't look at you and say, oh,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.303
thanks for crashing the economy

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.237
for a week.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:46.239
There is a kind of political penalty

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:46.840
to doing it.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.041
And I think they're acknowledging it

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:48.708
in a way,

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:49.943
and they've learned that lesson.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:50.510
One of the

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.711
I mean, it is so interesting

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:53.313
because some of the polling recently

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.414
had seemed to indicate that

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:55.548
perhaps the public

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:56.416
maybe wanted

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:57.383
the spending cuts

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.985
in exchange for a debt ceiling.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:00.053
But it seems like the deal

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.188
that was cut indicates that,

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:04.124
you know, the Democrats

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.492
kind of got slightly

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.627
more of what they wanted out

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:08.228
of this deal.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.930
Republicans were a little bit more upset.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.532
What do you make of Kevin

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.534
McCarthy actually making this move

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.869
and saying we got to cut a deal

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:16.970
no matter what?

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:17.170
Well,

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:17.971
I think, first off,

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:19.172
you can sit here and say,

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:20.440
I mean, a month ago

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.674
we were talking about

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:22.842
the Biden White House

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.477
and the Democrats at this position,

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.179
a clean debt limit increase. Right.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:27.647
So it is a huge win

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.315
for Kevin McCarthy today

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.783
to accomplish what he did today

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.153
and the end vote total.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:33.953
I know everybody's

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:34.821
going to try and spin it

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:35.588
on each side on it.

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:36.422
But the fact of the matter

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.992
is, 50% plus one is a win.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.593
It doesn't matter

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.529
who voted for it, who voted against it.

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:43.429
If you want to vote,

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.797
you one end of the day.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:45.632
And keep in mind,

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:46.332
the two people

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.534
that were two of the people

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:48.368
in this conversation,

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:51.037
Kevin McCarthy and Garret Graves,

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.740
they have worked in Congress

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.375
in the nineties.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:56.743
They worked for

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.211
Kevin McCarthy, worked for Bill Thomas,

00:02:58.211 --> 00:02:59.212
the first Republican Ways

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.280
and Means chair.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:00.847
Garret Graves

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:01.347
worked for

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.215
Billy Tauzin

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:02.916
from Louisiana,

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:04.150
the first Energy and Commerce

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.585
Republican chair.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.020
They know how the system works.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:07.787
These are serious.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.956
And so they sat down and they govern.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.091
I think you have to give McCarthy credit.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.394
I mean, he did get 149 Republicans.

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:15.562
He said he would get 150,

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:18.064
or somebody suggested he would also like

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.301
Joe Biden to like he's done this a bunch

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.836
and he did it under President Obama,

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:24.938
like his office.

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:25.672
And management

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.739
and budget director

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.608
who worked in House appropriations

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:30.310
for a very long time. L.Z.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.245
Granderson is also here with us.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.213
When Kevin McCarthy

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.514
was going through the crucible

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:36.883
to become speaker of the House, right.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:39.085
He went through 15 rounds

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.853
and he said this will

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.956
this will teach us how to govern.

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.157
Was he right?

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.526
No, but he has to say it right.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.627
He had to present the image

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:48.928
that this is their attempt

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:49.862
to try to

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.931
learn the lessons of the past and show

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.600
that it isn't just about saying no,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.201
but that we can say maybe,

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.337
which is language for compromise.

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.171
You know, listen,

00:03:58.171 --> 00:03:59.405
as you mentioned earlier,

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.640
we've had this conversation

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.041
a lot over the past decade.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.209
And I tell you what disturbs me

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.744
most about this conversation,

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:05.445
it isn't whether or not

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:07.647
it's a clean bill or a negative bill.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.048
It's whether or not you're going

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:09.882
after poor people

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.618
while trying to pass this bill.

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:13.319
And that's a reoccurring theme

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:14.320
Anderson, discussions.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.788
And I think as we continue

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.924
to have this discussion in 20, 25

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.092
after the election,

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.193
we're going to be trying to figure out

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:20.627
what are we going to do

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:21.361
with a debt ceiling.

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:23.463
And I'm afraid that once again,

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.530
poor people,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.599
people on welfare, people who need help,

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:27.934
are going to be the targets.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:28.735
And it would be good

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.069
if we could actually have a discussion

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:30.503
that lifts up

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.472
the entire budget beyond just hurting

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:33.273
poor people.

00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:34.240
Going forward.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:34.407
Well,

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:35.341
I think

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.576
another thing to keep in mind here

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:38.177
and Congressman Massie,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.545
you had a great point in that

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.948
this was a good exercise

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:42.982
to get us to the point

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:43.683
where we're actually going

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:45.418
to look at every program

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:46.419
in the form of the 13

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.620
appropriations bills.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.656
So once the 302 tube allocations

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.124
come out for the subcommittees,

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:52.859
that's that's a cap on it's

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:53.459
subcommittee

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.661
in the Appropriations Committee.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:55.662
But what I would like on

00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:57.063
this is for a long time,

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.431
and I have to say, people

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:59.632
always say that and the

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.503
process is not psychedelic in a.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:04.671
I do.

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:06.272
Now, McCarthy has to deliver on that.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:07.874
I appreciate your optimism

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:10.076
that Congress will start to govern, but

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:11.511
we will see if that happens.

